.TH ASSEMBLE 1 "October 11, 2026" "Vircon32 DevTools 26.04.24" "Vircon32 Development Tools"
.SH NAME
assemble \- Vircon32 assembler
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B assemble
.RB [ \-o
.IR output.vbin ]
.RB [ \-b ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-w ]
.RB [ \-g
.IR program | vbin | v32 ]
.I input.asm
.br
.B assemble
.B \-\-help
.br
.B assemble
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B assemble
converts one Vircon32 assembly source file into a machine-code
binary in the VBIN format, ready to be packed into a cartridge by
.BR packrom (1).
Its input is usually the output of
.BR compile (1),
but assembly can also be written by hand.
.PP
Assembly runs in four stages: lexer, preprocessor, parser and emitter.
Nothing is printed on success unless
.B \-v
is given. Exactly one input file is accepted.
.PP
Labels resolve to addresses in the cartridge program ROM, which starts
at
.B 0x20000000
(or in the BIOS program ROM at
.B 0x10000000
with
.BR \-b ).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " output.vbin"
Write the binary to
.IR output.vbin .
Without
.BR \-o ,
the output path is the input path with its extension replaced by
.IR .vbin .
.TP
.B \-b
Assemble the code as a BIOS: addresses start at the BIOS program ROM
.RB ( 0x10000000 ).
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose: report the output path, each stage, and the size of the
produced binary in words and bytes.
.TP
.B \-w
Inhibit all warnings.
.TP
.BI \-g " reference"
Also write a debug information file,
.IR output.vbin.debug ,
relating every ROM position to its source file and line, and listing
the labels. The
.I reference
says how addresses in it are given:
.RS
.TP
.B program
in words, relative to the start of the program;
.TP
.B vbin
in bytes, relative to the start of the VBIN file;
.TP
.B v32
in bytes, relative to the start of the packed cartridge file.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the assembler version and author, and exit.
.TP
.B \-s
Accepted for compatibility; has no effect.
.PP
Any other argument that starts with
.B \-
is an error.
.SH ASSEMBLY LANGUAGE
.SS Lines
Each line holds one instruction, one data directive, one label
declaration or one preprocessor directive. Everything from a
.B ;
to the end of the line is a comment. Instruction names, register
names and directive keywords are not case-sensitive.
.SS Labels
A label is a name that starts with an underscore. It is declared with a
colon and used as an operand, where it stands for the address of what
follows its declaration:
.PP
.RS
.nf
_loop:
    wait
    jmp _loop
.fi
.RE
.SS Literals
Integers are written in decimal or in hexadecimal with
.B 0x
(a leading
.B \-
negates); floats have a decimal point
.RI ( 1.5 ,
.IR \-0.25 ;
no exponent); characters in single quotes
.RI ( \(aqA\(aq )
are their code;
.B true
and
.B false
are 1 and 0. Strings, in double quotes, are allowed only in
.B string
and
.B datafile
directives. Escapes in characters and strings are
.BR \en ", " \er ", " \et ", " \e\e ", " \e\(aq ", " \e\(dq
and
.BI \ex HH\c
\&.
.SS Registers
.B R0
to
.BR R15 ,
all general purpose. Five have alternate names that reflect what
some instructions use them for:
.PP
.RS
.TS
l l l.
CR	R11	count register (string instructions)
SR	R12	source register (string instructions)
DR	R13	destination register (string instructions)
BP	R14	base pointer (stack frames)
SP	R15	stack pointer
.TE
.RE
.SS Memory operands
A memory operand is written in brackets:
.BI [ address ]\c
,
.BI [ register ]\c
, or
.BI [ register + offset ]
.RB ( \- " is also allowed)."
Memory is addressed in 32-bit words.
.SS Instructions
Every instruction takes one cycle; the CPU runs 250000 cycles per
frame (15 million per second). Comparisons and conversions overwrite
their first operand with the result: there are no flags.
.I reg
is a register,
.I imm
an immediate value or label,
.I mem
a memory operand.
.PP
.TS
lb l l.
HLT		stop the CPU
WAIT		wait for the next frame
JMP	imm | reg	jump
CALL	imm | reg	push return address, jump
RET		return from a CALL
JT	reg, imm | reg	jump if reg is not 0
JF	reg, imm | reg	jump if reg is 0
IEQ INE IGT IGE ILT ILE	reg, imm | reg	integer compare: reg = 1/0
FEQ FNE FGT FGE FLT FLE	reg, imm | reg	float compare: reg = 1/0
MOV	reg, imm | reg | mem	load
MOV	mem, reg	store
LEA	reg, [reg+offset]	load the address itself
PUSH	reg	push onto the stack
POP	reg	pop from the stack
IN	reg, port	read an I/O port
OUT	port, imm | reg	write an I/O port
MOVS		copy CR words [SR] to [DR]
SETS		set CR words at [DR] to SR
CMPS	reg	compare CR words
CIF CFI	reg	int to float, float to int
CIB CFB	reg	int or float to bool 0/1
NOT	reg	bitwise not
AND OR XOR	reg, imm | reg	bitwise and, or, xor
BNOT	reg	boolean not
SHL	reg, imm | reg	shift left (< 0: right)
IADD ISUB IMUL	reg, imm | reg	integer + \- \(mu
IDIV IMOD	reg, imm | reg	integer / and %
IMIN IMAX	reg, imm | reg	integer min, max
ISGN IABS	reg	integer negate, absolute
FADD FSUB FMUL	reg, imm | reg	float + \- \(mu
FDIV FMOD	reg, imm | reg	float / and remainder
FMIN FMAX	reg, imm | reg	float min, max
FSGN FABS	reg	float negate, absolute
FLR CEIL ROUND	reg	round down, up, nearest
SIN ACOS	reg	sine, arc cosine
ATAN2	reg, reg	arc tangent of reg1/reg2
LOG	reg	natural logarithm
POW	reg, reg	reg1 to the power reg2
.TE
.PP
.BR MOVS ,
.B SETS
and
.B CMPS
repeat until
.B CR
reaches 0, advancing
.B SR
and
.B DR
one word per cycle;
.B CMPS
stops early at the first difference, leaving
.I reg
as the difference between the two words ([DR] minus [SR]),
or 0 when all are equal. Division by zero, an arc cosine
or logarithm outside its domain, a stack overflow or underflow, and an
access to a nonexistent address or port raise a hardware error, which
stops the program and makes the BIOS show an error screen.
.SS Ports
.B IN
and
.B OUT
take an I/O port by name:
.BR TIM_ *
(timer),
.B RNG_CurrentValue
(random generator),
.BR GPU_ *,
.BR SPU_ *,
.BR INP_ *
(gamepads),
.BR CAR_ *
(cartridge) and
.B MEM_Connected
(memory card) \(em for example
.BR GPU_DrawingPointX " or " INP_GamepadButtonA .
A numeric port number is accepted with a warning. The values with
special meaning for
.B OUT
have names too:
.BR GPUCommand_ *
.RB ( GPUCommand_ClearScreen ,
.BR GPUCommand_DrawRegion ,
\&...),
.BR GPUBlendingMode_ *,
.BR SPUCommand_ *
and
.BR SPUChannelState_ *.
.SS Data directives
.TP
.BI integer " value" ", ..."
One word per value.
.TP
.BI float " value" ", ..."
One word per value.
.TP
.BI string " \(dqtext\(dq"
One word per character, plus a terminating 0.
.TP
.BI pointer " label" ", ..."
The address of each label.
.TP
.BI datafile " \(dqpath\(dq"
The contents of a binary file, whose size must be a multiple of 4
bytes. The path is used as written, so a relative path is relative to
the directory
.B assemble
is run from.
.SS Preprocessor
Directives begin with
.BR % :
.TP
.BI %include " \(dqpath\(dq"
Insert another assembly file, looked for in the directory of the
including file.
.TP
.BI %define " name value"
Replace every later occurrence of
.I name
with the rest of the line.
.TP
.BI %undef " name"
.TP
.BR %ifdef ", " %ifndef ", " %else ", " %endif
Conditional assembly on whether a name is defined.
.TP
.BR %error ", " %warning
Stop with an error, or print a warning, with the given message.
.SH OUTPUT FORMAT
A VBIN file is a 12-byte header \(em the 8 characters
.B V32\-VBIN
followed by the number of 32-bit words as a little-endian integer
\(em and then the words themselves.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Problems are reported on standard error with the file and line they
were found at, and a failed run ends with
.PP
.RS
.nf
assemble: error: description
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The binary was written (or
.BR \-\-help / \-\-version
was given).
.TP
.B 1
Any error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
compile game.c \-o obj/game.asm
assemble obj/game.asm \-o obj/game.vbin
.fi
.RE
.PP
With an optimizer in between:
.PP
.RS
.nf
compile game.c \-o obj/game.asm
v32opt obj/game.asm \-o obj/gameOpt.asm \-O1
assemble obj/gameOpt.asm \-o obj/game.vbin
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR compile (1),
.BR disassemble (1),
.BR packrom (1),
.BR v32opt (1),
.BR vircon32 (3v32)
.SH BUGS
The usage summary lists a
.B \-\-debugmode
option, but the assembler does not recognize it and stops with
\(lqunrecognized command line option\(rq.
.SH AUTHOR
The Vircon32 assembler was written by Javier Carracedo
.RI ( Carra ).
